A||||8||MooCow||How reliable are the dukes?||||5||
Z||000000||MooCow||02-17-2001||01:03 AM||||If this topic has been coverd for give me.  I am looking for a winter Fiero for next year now so I have all summer to fix it and make it reliable.  I want to know how  reliable the duke really is?  Is it different from 84-86 or what?  What should I look for?  I am looking at an 84 stick on tuesday and later on this week I'm looking at an 86 auto. Are the 4cyl sticks more reliable or the same as the v6 Fiero's?  I need the car to be a relaible Fiero only for winter months.  Or should I just look for a V6 Fiero?<BR>Sorry for all the questions.<P>                              Thanks Ken<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/kensig_1.jpg"> ||||reg||5
Z||000001||LuckyTheyWereEverMade||02-17-2001||01:38 AM||||Your question is easy to ask, but not so easy to answer.  The reliability of a 15 year old car depends on a lot of stuff.<P>The iron dukes are very reliable.  They were used in tons of GM cars because they were good on gas, cheap, and reliable.  The only drawback is that it's not a performance engine.  Modifications can get more power but at the expense of reliability once you get above 130 hp.  <P>The V6 has 140hp to begin with, so if you want to drive fast, this is the only choice.  On the other hand, a 5spd stick shift 2.5L duke can get 30 mpg or better.  Even an '84 duke automatic, although slowest of all Fieros, is still fun to drive.  The duke equipped Fieros have more forgiving handling characteristics.<P>All of this now depends on how the car was maintained.  Over the years, parts on even the best cars fail.  When an owner ignores the problems and keeps driving, other parts fail under the stress of a poorly running car.  Given most 2.5L cars have at least 90K miles on them by now, the way the car was maintained is key to reliability now.  If the owner does not have reciepts for LOTS of repairs, then you may be in for replacement of a wide array of sensors, belts, and hoses.  Those sensors really added up in cost!<P>Drive the cars.  If they idle with little or no variance, that's actually a good sign.  If the car accelerates smoothly regardless of how the gas pedal is used, then that's another good sign.  If the car has hesitation, then it might not be the one you want.  <P>Also look for coolant leaks on the bottom of the car, rear end and front.  Open the decklid and look at the thermostat housing.  Then look below it at the top of the transmission directly below.  If you see coolant in a little puddle here, there's probably a leak above.  Most part stores no longer stock thermostat housings.<P>Check the oil and watch the car on start up.  It may make some smoke on start up, but it should stop after less than 5 minutes at idle.  If the car is driven, it should not take anywhere near as long for the exhaust to be all but invisible.  If the idle is uneven, listen to the car while standing near the exhaust behind the car.  After the car has been running for more than five minutes, If you hear deep tones like a missfire and see smoke at the same time then you have another possible bad sign.<P>These observations may help you to aviod a car with bad sensors, cylinder head/head gasket, and ignitiion problems.  <P>Next rev the engine while looking at it from the drivers side.  Do you see smoke comming from the engine when you rev it hard?  That could be transmission, engine, distributor, gaskets, or an exhaust leak.  It might be easy to fix any of these problems, but finding them can be frustrating!<P>I hope this helps, but I must say that whenever I buy a car it always needs work no matter what brand or age.  You should expect to spend a little money on the car right away.  As they say "A stitch in time saves nine"  ||||reg||1
Z||000002||rnoswal||02-17-2001||01:38 AM||||Go for the V6. The Iron duke is pretty reliable. I got a very used 85 4 cyl last year and after just a little maintenance, it has always started in the mornings. But it is under powered and just a little noisey. The V6 will run the AC easily. Plugs are a little hard to change on the V6, but they don't have to be changed too often. My 4 cyl has 164k miles and doesn't look like it has ever been taken apart. But instead of rebuilding it I am going to put a 2.8 v6 in it. In the long run it will be worth it for me and for you too! Of course that is just my opinion and I could be wrong!<P>Russ ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Gold-86SE||02-17-2001||01:57 AM||||<BR>I drive an 84 SE during inclement weather, mostly because it does not have an ac and this is my first South Tejas winter with it.<P>With that in mind, I purchased this Fiero:<BR>A) because it is a Fiero,<BR>B) because it <B><I>is</I></B> an 84,<BR>C) the interior was almost perfect (no rips, no tears, no broken dash, just limited warpage on the console)<BR>D) it has PL, PW, PM, no sunroof, cruise, or rear defrost,<BR>E) it had just over 80k,<BR>F) it was $350.<P>Sad fact is that I had to replace the Duck because of a head and/or head gasket problem, but the replacement engine was only 100 bucks, so I am not complaining.<P>All else told I have invested about $6-700 in the car since January 2000, that includes brand new rubber, but she still needs some work. A new paint job would be nice, and so would some working head light motors.  I just do what I can as I earn money.<P>If you look for a Duke, I suggest an 84 because that is the only 4-banger Fiero that has any chance of being loaded-with-options.  And with a 4.10 tranny the 84 Duck has decent acceleration, but nothing compared to my 4-speed 86 V6 SE.<P>Final note: it is my preference, but I would definately choose a stick over the auto when driving an Iron Duke Fiero.<P>Just my $0.02<P>Forgot to add that when I first purchased that 84, I would not drive it further than about 10-30 miles per week.  Now, having spent time with her, I frequently put 170-225 miles per week on her and she has been great.  It just has taken some time to work out her bugs.<P>------------------<BR>84 SE 4.10 4 Speed (Cold and Rain Daily Driver)<BR>85 GT Automatic (Restoration Project)<BR>86 SE 4 Speed (Fair Weather Daily Driver)<BR>87 SC 5 Speed (Not Running, V8 Conversion?)<p>[This message has been edited by Gold-86SE (edited 02-17-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||Old Lar||02-17-2001||08:07 AM||||There was an iron Duke at th Louisville show that has 419000 miles on it. The first time I saw this car at a show was several years ago and it had over 300000 miles on it at that time. I think it has had 3 clutches, but the engine has not been rebuilt. The big factor is engine/car maintenance.||||reg||3
Z||000005||Formula88||02-17-2001||11:52 AM||||I know LZeitgeist had an 88 coupe with about 250,000 miles on it's Tech4.  Personally, I'd go for the later year with the Tech4 rather than an earlier Duke.  They're both good, but the Tech4 is a little more advanced, a bit smoother, etc.  Not going to have much power, but it will get good mileage. I wouldn't even consider a 4cyl auto. The 2.5 really needs a 4 or 5 speed to have any kind of acceptable performance.  And the mileage is much better too.||||reg||6
Z||000006||sqoach||02-17-2001||03:07 PM||||some of you guys need to think about what you said. v6 has more power, acceleration, speed. why would you want that in the winter? you have to drive more carefully during this time of year. i would go for a duke because it's probably cheaper, and gets better gas mileage. save your money for your nice car.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>just my 2 1/2 cents.<P>------------------<BR>sqoach@yahoo.com<P>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/mysig2.JPG"> <BR>84 SE (project car?)<BR>85 Coupe (parts car)<BR>86 SC (daily driver)||||reg||1
Z||000007||MooCow||02-18-2001||01:01 AM||||Hey thanks for the wonderful replies.<BR>No I do not want a V6 really cause I think I would get into an accident.  The only reason I want an auto verses the manual is os I don't have to mess with the clutch.  There is an 84 with 90,000 and a mint interior and the paint looked good.  4-speed stick and the clutch felt new.  It is at a dealer and they want $2,000!!  I am testing it on tuesday to see how good of shape it is in.  How much are the 84 Fiero's really worth?  I know its worth whatever I would pay for it but seriously what would you pay for a Fiero like that?  O yea it also has power windows and locks and tilt, not sure on the AC.<P>                            Thanks Ken<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/kensig_1.jpg"> ||||reg||10
Z||000008||Gold-86SE||02-18-2001||10:16 AM||||<BR>just consider how much money you would have to 'invest' in the car, if it did not run, or ran poorly.  if paying 2000 or near it, allows you NOT to spend money on repairs then you may be money ahead.  It all depends on what you are willing to outlay.<P>If it runs great, handles great, and looks fantastic, then buy it.<P>Just my approximate $2000.||||reg||1
